    source: "Kahneman and Tversky (1973); the taxi problem (Tversky and Kahneman, 1982). 85% of taxis are green and 15% blue. A witness who is 80% reliable says they saw a blue taxi. The typical answer is 80%; the correct answer is about 41%.",
    celebre: "Casscells, Schoenberger and Graboys (1978) put a question to 60 doctors and students at Harvard Medical School: a disease with a prevalence of 1 in 1,000, a test with 5% false positives; what is the probability that a patient who tests positive is ill? Nearly half answer 95%. Only about 18% give the right answer, about 2%.",
    distinction: "The prosecutor’s fallacy is a special case of base rate neglect: it inverts a conditional probability, taking P(evidence | innocent) for P(innocent | evidence)."
