    parade: "Evaluate each option by its end result, not relative to a reference point. Ask yourself: if I had nothing today, would I choose to buy this position?",
    source: "Kahneman and Tversky (1979), prospect theory. Tversky and Kahneman (1992) estimate the loss aversion coefficient at about 2.25.",
    celebre: "Pope and Schweitzer (2011), on more than 2.5 million putts on the US professional tour (PGA): at equal distance, golfers sink about 2 percentage points fewer of their putts for birdie (gaining a stroke) than of their putts for par (avoiding losing a stroke).",
    distinction: "Loss aversion is the general principle; the endowment effect is one consequence of it, applied to objects we own."
